Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Alternative: Loading Records into a HashMap
The current find() method reads the entire file sequentially whenever a search is performed.

An alternative is to load all bus-pass records into a HashMap when the application starts.

The HashMap stores:
- Key: Pass ID.
- Value: Complete bus-pass record.
This allows records to be retrieved directly using their pass IDs.
1. Java Implementation Using HashMap

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;

public class PassIndex {

    private Map<String, String[]> passMap =
            new HashMap<>();

    public void load(Path file) throws IOException {

        try (BufferedReader r =
                     Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {

            String line;

            while ((line = r.readLine()) != null) {

                String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);

                // Skip damaged records
                if (fields.length != 5) {
                    continue;
                }

                passMap.put(fields[0], fields);
            }
        }
    }

    public String[] find(String passId) {

        return passMap.get(passId);
    }
}



Explanation:
1. The file is read once when the application starts.
2. Each valid record is inserted into the HashMap.
3. The pass ID is used as the key.
4. When searching, get() retrieves the matching record directly.
5. Damaged records are skipped during loading.
2. Complexity Comparison
Let:
- \(n\) = number of bus-pass records.
- \(q\) = number of searches.
Operation	Sequential File Search	HashMap Search
Initial loading	Not required	O(n)
One search	O(n)	O(1) average
q searches	O(q × n)	O(n + q) average
Additional memory	O(1)	O(n)

Sequential Search:

For every search, the program scans the file again.
\[
T(n)=O(q\times n)
\]

HashMap Search:

The file is scanned once to build the HashMap. Subsequent searches use the HashMap.
\[
T(n)=O(n+q)
\]

HashMap retrieval is O(1) on average, although worst-case lookup can be O(n).
3. Space Complexity Comparison
Approach	Space Complexity	Explanation
Sequential file search	O(1) additional space	Only one record is processed at a time
HashMap	O(n)	All valid records are stored in memory

The HashMap approach requires more memory because it stores all records in memory. However, it significantly reduces repeated file-reading operations.
4. Final Comparison and Decision
For a small bus-pass file, sequential searching is simple and requires very little memory.

For a large bus-pass file with frequent searches, HashMap provides better performance because the records are loaded only once.

Final decision: I would use sequential searching when the number of searches is small and memory usage must be minimized. If the transport office performs frequent searches on thousands of records, I would choose HashMap because its average search complexity is O(1).

Additional Implementation: Listing Passes by Route
The supplied starter code does not include the route-listing operation from Task A1. To complete all three required file-store operations, the following method can be added to PassStore without changing the existing methods.

public void listByRoute(int searchRoute)
        throws IOException {

    try (BufferedReader r =
                 Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {

        String line;
        boolean found = false;

        while ((line = r.readLine()) != null) {

            String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);

            if (fields.length != 5) {
                continue;
            }

            try {

                int route = Integer.parseInt(fields[3]);

                if (route == searchRoute) {

                    System.out.println(
                        Arrays.toString(fields)
                    );

                    found = true;
                }

            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid route record skipped");
            }
        }

        if (!found) {
            System.out.println(
                "No bus passes found for this route"
            );
        }
    }
}




